# Always cite workspace code

When showing or referring to code that already exists in the workspace, you MUST use a code reference fence so the chat shows a jump-link control. Clicking that control jumps to the file and highlights the range.

The fence info-string is `startLine:endLine:filepath` only — no language tag:

    ```12:15:src/app.ts
    export function foo() {
      return 1;
    }
    ```

## Required format

- Header is `startLine:endLine:filepath` only. Do not add `python`, `ts`, or any language tag.
- Include at least one real line of code in the body. Empty fences do not render a jump.
- Put the opening backticks at column 0 (not indented inside a list).
- In sentences, mention paths with single backticks. Put the clickable jump on the fence, never on inline triple-backticks.

## When you already know the location

If this turn’s `Read` / `Grep` / other file tools already gave a verified filepath and line range, cite that immediately. Do not invent line numbers.

## When you do not have a verified location

If you are about to show existing code (or the user wants to jump / open / find it) and you do **not** have a verified filepath + line range from this turn, call MCP `resolve_code_jump` on server `plugin-chat-jump-links-jump` **before** writing the answer.

Pass:

- `snippet`: 1–8 literal lines (strip `... existing code ...` placeholders)
- `workspace_roots`: absolute workspace folder paths from this session
- optional `hint_path`, `hint_symbol`, `glob`

Then paste `best.citation_fence_header` unchanged, with the matched body. Do not rewrite the header.

If several matches are close, cite `best` and list other `filepath:startLine` in prose. If there is no match, say so and do not fake a citation.

## New code

Proposed code that is **not** in the repo uses a normal language fence (for example ` ```python `). Do not invent a path for it.
